00:48:42.520 --> 00:48:46.680
you love that I'm doing, so I know I can continue&nbsp;
it and what are the bottom three things that I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:46.680 --> 00:48:50.800
improve on? Three things is definitely doable,&nbsp;
if not three, just even do one, you can do T1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:50.800 --> 00:48:54.200
B1, and I just have it in my calendar. I think&nbsp;
more things you can just have automatic in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:54.200 --> 00:48:59.160
calendar, the better. So I just have my calendar,&nbsp;
ask the team T3, B3 every quarter. And so right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:59.160 --> 00:49:02.640
now in your business, if you're in the junior&nbsp;
department, if you're the leader of the company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:02.640 --> 00:49:07.320
or even in relationships, whatever it is, just&nbsp;
go ask your partner, ask someone that you trust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:07.320 --> 00:49:10.760
and respect, "Hey, what [are] the top three things&nbsp;
I'm doing? What are the bottom three?" And do that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:10.760 --> 00:49:13.360
regularly and you'll be able to keep improving.
Tim Ferriss: What, can you give any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:13.360 --> 00:49:17.760
examples of real T3, B3?
Noah Kagan: So on my desk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:17.760 --> 00:49:21.480
I print mine out, because I want to see it and I&nbsp;
want to know what I'm — what you're doing, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:21.480 --> 00:49:25.040
you're probably going to continue, but what you&nbsp;
can improve is cool. So the team gave me feedback&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:25.040 --> 00:49:31.320
that they want more autonomy, that I'm too much&nbsp;
up in their ass. And this is the funniest part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:31.320 --> 00:49:35.627
everyone's like, "I'm not a micromanager." I'm&nbsp;
like, "Dude, everyone who says that is," okay.&nbsp;

00:49:35.627 --> 00:49:39.080
Tim Ferriss: It's kind of like if you see on a&nbsp;
dating app, anyone who says "No drama," you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:39.080 --> 00:49:45.000
like, "Oh, you love drama." Huge red flag.
Noah Kagan: Huge red flag. So they wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:45.000 --> 00:49:51.200
more autonomy, and then it asked for an example,&nbsp;
what's an example, or what's a moment? Give me a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:51.200 --> 00:49:55.520
solution that we make sure that, as it comes&nbsp;
to it. So for instance, with AppSumo.com,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:57.080 --> 00:50:01.240
I love going through our credit card bill.&nbsp;
Our credit card bills, sometimes November,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:01.240 --> 00:50:05.080
because Black Friday it's about a million&nbsp;
dollars, almost a million dollars and normally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:05.080 --> 00:50:07.480
it can vary between three and 500 —
Tim Ferriss: That's on customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:07.480 --> 00:50:11.480
acquisition, paid acquisition?
Noah Kagan: Facebook or Amazon bill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:11.480 --> 00:50:15.160
50k a month, a lot of different ads.
Tim Ferriss: Just so I understand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:15.160 --> 00:50:18.360
Amazon bill, you're talking about AWS?
Noah Kagan: AWS, yeah. So I was going through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:18.360 --> 00:50:21.680
every transaction and it'd be a $9 transaction —
Tim Ferriss: I would love if your Amazon bill is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:21.680 --> 00:50:26.240
just shit your employees are buying as gifts. AWS.
Noah Kagan: I mean, well, as you get bigger you'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:26.240 --> 00:50:28.680
also have checks and balances. So there's&nbsp;
different, someone actually tried to scam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:28.680 --> 00:50:32.200
us for 30k a few weeks ago, which is super&nbsp;
impressive. But I was going through these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:32.200 --> 00:50:37.510
credit card bills and if we talked about coaches,&nbsp;
we have a CFO coach for the VP of finance. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:37.510 --> 00:50:42.120
was the CFO of Mailchimp, pretty good job, pretty&nbsp;
cool company. We're in a meeting and she's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:42.120 --> 00:50:48.280
"Noah, we talk about autonomy, we talk about&nbsp;
trust. Do you think going through the credit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:48.280 --> 00:50:53.080
card transactions and messaging people about a&nbsp;
$10 purchase is really autonomy and not trusting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:53.080 --> 00:50:56.960
your VP of finance, Kevin, who can do this?" And&nbsp;
I was like, "Good point." I stopped looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:56.960 --> 00:51:02.280
credit card bills. So that was one of them.
The other B3 from this quarter was around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:02.280 --> 00:51:05.840
longer-term vision. I'm much more of&nbsp;
a tactician and I like getting action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:05.840 --> 00:51:09.840
I like getting movement. And the team was like,&nbsp;
"You need to chill out and think longer for us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:09.840 --> 00:51:14.520
and come back with some type of longer plan." And&nbsp;
so the last six months of the year, it was really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:14.520 --> 00:51:18.120
thinking about what do we want to do in the next&nbsp;
three years. I've been so focused in business and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:18.120 --> 00:51:23.547
generally about one-year segments. So we spent&nbsp;
so much time and now we have a 2026 vision.&nbsp;

00:51:23.547 --> 00:51:27.400
Tim Ferriss: I think this is also very common,&nbsp;
it's not true for everyone, but very common for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:27.400 --> 00:51:30.027
people who've done a lot of bootstrapping.
Noah Kagan: Yeah.&nbsp;

00:51:30.027 --> 00:51:34.600
Tim Ferriss: More so than people who've raised&nbsp;
a bunch of money. Where a board understandably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:34.600 --> 00:51:43.120
wants to know what the hell your long-term plans&nbsp;
are, because they want to realize their return.&nbsp;

00:51:43.120 --> 00:51:45.480
Noah Kagan: Equity. And that was the feedback&nbsp;
I got from the team and they're like, "Get out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:45.480 --> 00:51:50.480
of the day-to-day, let us do it, we got it." And&nbsp;
just start moving the process forward. So it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:50.480 --> 00:51:53.760
thinking about talking to customers, talking&nbsp;
to partners, thinking about ourselves. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:53.760 --> 00:51:58.320
do we like doing? What are we great at and where&nbsp;
can we be in three years? And then bringing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:58.320 --> 00:52:01.600
back to the team, incorporating them, getting&nbsp;
them buy-in. That was something I got feedback&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:01.600 --> 00:52:06.280
in the past like, "Hey, you're just making&nbsp;
decisions without us." So by the end of the year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:06.280 --> 00:52:10.227
I think mid-November now we have a three-year&nbsp;
vision and base camps towards that vision.&nbsp;

00:52:10.227 --> 00:52:11.908
Tim Ferriss: Basecamp's&nbsp;
project management, you mean?&nbsp;

00:52:11.908 --> 00:52:16.160
Noah Kagan: Not necessarily Basecamp.com, but&nbsp;
base camps, specifically, what are milestones.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:16.160 --> 00:52:20.160
So our destination is to be able to distribute&nbsp;
software tools for entrepreneurs. That's what we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:20.160 --> 00:52:24.680
love doing, I love promoting things. And so how do&nbsp;
we be the number one place to distribute software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:24.680 --> 00:52:29.600
tools, not just deals, but software tools and then&nbsp;
what are the different key milestones numerically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:29.600 --> 00:52:33.120
as well as from what we want to do in the&nbsp;
business within these separate years, to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:33.120 --> 00:52:37.480
able to get there in 2026? So that was feedback&nbsp;
directly from the team, that they told me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:37.480 --> 00:52:40.548
and then we do this for everyone in the company.
Tim Ferriss: The top three, bottom three.&nbsp;

00:52:40.548 --> 00:52:43.440
Noah Kagan: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: How do people track that or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:43.440 --> 00:52:49.800
how do people keep it present? Because it is one&nbsp;
thing having just come off of an off-site myself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:49.800 --> 00:52:54.120
and I actually think we did a good job with this,&nbsp;
because we sort of assigned ownership next steps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:54.120 --> 00:52:58.720
and then did working sessions during the off-site.&nbsp;
Okay, let's actually get these things in motion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:58.720 --> 00:53:05.280
because it's great to have a bunch of meals, have&nbsp;
some wine, walk around, talk about big ideas. Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:05.280 --> 00:53:11.200
we're so excited, let's take a team photo&nbsp;
next to the paper on the wall and then right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:11.200 --> 00:53:16.680
back to status quo. How do people keep&nbsp;
these top three, bottom three present,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:16.680 --> 00:53:20.028
or maybe a better way to word it is,&nbsp;
how do they keep themselves accountable?&nbsp;

00:53:20.028 --> 00:53:24.400
Noah Kagan: A few things that work for us, so&nbsp;
one is scorecards. Everyone gets a scorecard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:24.400 --> 00:53:28.640
If you're an AppSumo, you get a scorecard and&nbsp;
so I can show you an example of it. I focus on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:28.640 --> 00:53:31.360
the leadership scorecard. So I have a company&nbsp;
scorecard. What are our top three things we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:31.360 --> 00:53:37.120
trying to do in Q1? And Seann, he's our head of&nbsp;
revenue, Ilona, she's our COO. They each have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:37.120 --> 00:53:40.800
a scorecard with just what are your top three&nbsp;
things based on what we're trying to do in Q1.&nbsp;

00:53:40.800 --> 00:53:43.880
Tim Ferriss: This would be for Q1?
Noah Kagan: Yeah. And so that is a helpful way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:43.880 --> 00:53:46.440
where maybe it's something they want to improve,&nbsp;
or maybe it's just something that they need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:46.440 --> 00:53:50.920
work on, or it's just an outcome that we want and&nbsp;
then we agree on what those are. They propose it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:50.920 --> 00:53:52.788
I review it and then we decide it.
Tim Ferriss: Got it.&nbsp;

00:53:52.788 --> 00:53:55.640
Noah Kagan: It doesn't mean necessarily,&nbsp;
hey, it's going to be one of their B3s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:55.640 --> 00:53:59.160
but that for me, is the three things that&nbsp;
they want to get better at, or they want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:59.160 --> 00:54:03.600
accomplish. The other thing I would say, it&nbsp;
sounded like you guys had a planning session&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:03.600 --> 00:54:08.080
and all this stuff. We had, I told you we have a&nbsp;
three-year vision and then we have all this stuff.&nbsp;

00:54:08.080 --> 00:54:12.680
But on Monday I was with one of my coaches, Dan&nbsp;
Putt, from Reboot, and I was talking to Dan, I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:12.680 --> 00:54:15.880
like, "I don't know if we're all on the same page&nbsp;
what we're doing, because I'm not really clear."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:15.880 --> 00:54:22.160
He's like, "Well, if you're not clear, they're&nbsp;
definitely not clear." And I was like, "Oh, that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:22.160 --> 00:54:26.280
interesting." It comes from the top, how are you&nbsp;
leading by example? If I'm not clear where we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:26.280 --> 00:54:30.160
going to go necessarily in the next quarter, does&nbsp;
everyone else know? So we had a meeting, so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:30.160 --> 00:54:34.920
day I was like, "Everyone needs to bring their&nbsp;
top scorecard projects. We need to align on them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:34.920 --> 00:54:38.440
make sure they're tied to the main thing and&nbsp;
have a one pager around it." Had a meeting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:38.440 --> 00:54:42.760
next day, everyone brought their stuff, and then&nbsp;
we have a one-pager that ties all this together.&nbsp;

00:54:42.760 --> 00:54:47.640
And so now being very, very clear, what's our&nbsp;
number goals? What's our key projects? By the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:47.640 --> 00:54:51.000
this is something that's counterintuitive&nbsp;
in businesses, is if there's not fighting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:51.000 --> 00:54:55.280
you're not really getting the best ideas. I&nbsp;
want more people saying no. The best people at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:55.280 --> 00:54:58.560
Facebook when I was there were always saying&nbsp;
no to Mark. I was always like, "Yeah, Mark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:58.560 --> 00:55:03.200
that was a pretty good idea." That's maybe why&nbsp;
I got fired. But over time I've realized that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:03.200 --> 00:55:08.480
challenging respectfully is very healthy. And so&nbsp;
you ask about scorecard, you ask about feedback&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:08.480 --> 00:55:12.320
and all that. It's ask for feedback, really&nbsp;
be clear in what success is. That's what a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:12.320 --> 00:55:17.240
scorecard is, it's here is success for me with&nbsp;
our work and then you can be able to measure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:17.240 --> 00:55:19.480
against that in a very clear amount of time.
Tim Ferriss: We are going to come back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:19.480 --> 00:55:26.360
saying no pretty quickly, because a big part&nbsp;
of enabling yourself to say yes to the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:26.360 --> 00:55:31.760
that matter is being able and equipped to say&nbsp;
no. So I do want to come back to that. Other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:31.760 --> 00:55:38.800
frameworks that you would like to mention?
Noah Kagan: The other one I do, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:38.800 --> 00:55:44.480
changed everything for me in the past two years,&nbsp;
especially as a CEO, was a weekly review. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:44.480 --> 00:55:48.400
I only have three questions and really noticing&nbsp;
what you actually do. A lot of people are like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:48.400 --> 00:55:51.200
"I have all these frameworks." And then you ask&nbsp;
them a year later like, "Yeah, I don't use it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:51.200 --> 00:55:54.880
again." So maybe they've evolved it, but really&nbsp;
it's not sticking. And so this is one that sticks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:54.880 --> 00:56:00.920
with me and I could show you, it comes up on&nbsp;
my phone. So every week I get a Slack bot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:00.920 --> 00:56:05.360
here it is, you can see. So it says "Complete&nbsp;
your Friday weekly review." So today's Friday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:05.360 --> 00:56:09.800
as we're chatting, you can click on it and there's&nbsp;
three questions. And the three questions are very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:09.800 --> 00:56:14.680
very simple. "How was the week, one through&nbsp;
five, and why?" And this is you could do whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:14.680 --> 00:56:18.188
you're a CEO or whether you're a junior person.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, this goes to a Google Form.&nbsp;

00:56:18.188 --> 00:56:21.064
Noah Kagan: Yeah. And so we track&nbsp;
it, we track the ratings every week.&nbsp;

00:56:21.064 --> 00:56:24.680
Tim Ferriss: So one is sucked, five is fantastic.
Noah Kagan: So this week was a five, this week&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:24.680 --> 00:56:28.240
crushed. And then that's thinking about, okay,&nbsp;
well, what made this week a five? What happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:28.240 --> 00:56:32.440
this week? I got to hang out with you. We had lack&nbsp;
of clarity on Monday, on Tuesday, we dialed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:32.440 --> 00:56:36.360
our Q1 planning and everyone was super hyped&nbsp;
on that. I finished executive compensation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:36.360 --> 00:56:40.400
which was challenging, about how much people get&nbsp;
paid. So this week was a five and then explaining,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:40.400 --> 00:56:43.720
line adding, all those different things.&nbsp;
And it's not always, I just bullet point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:43.720 --> 00:56:47.720
I don't necessarily need to write stories. Number&nbsp;
two thing, especially, is my leadership style,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:47.720 --> 00:56:51.022
I noticed I was very inconsistent.
Tim Ferriss: Were you consistent?&nbsp;

00:56:51.022 --> 00:56:53.240
Noah Kagan: Yeah. So my number two question&nbsp;
is, "Was I consistent this week, one through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:53.240 --> 00:56:58.640
five?" And basically because I'd go into a meeting&nbsp;
and I would just start fucking ripping on people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:58.640 --> 00:57:01.880
I was in meeting with Mitchell and Olman, and I'm&nbsp;
just shitting all over them. I'm like, "Your ideas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:01.880 --> 00:57:07.960
are sucky." And I wasn't coming with solutions.&nbsp;
That week was a three. That was not a good idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:07.960 --> 00:57:11.707
because as a leader you want consistent&nbsp;
experience for the people you work with.&nbsp;

00:57:11.707 --> 00:57:16.040
Tim Ferriss: And you're being consistent based on&nbsp;
feedback you've received from the team and your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:16.040 --> 00:57:18.560
top three, bottom three? Is that sort of a —
Noah Kagan: A little bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:19.160 --> 00:57:21.748
this is more my own subjective opinion.
Tim Ferriss: Got it. This is how you feel?&nbsp;

00:57:21.748 --> 00:57:26.200
Noah Kagan: Yeah, and I found this really,&nbsp;
really helpful. And then the last question is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:26.200 --> 00:57:30.400
"What are my top three for next week?" So it&nbsp;
makes me be very more focused about, okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:30.400 --> 00:57:34.520
I have a singular goal, we have Q1 priorities.&nbsp;
Now I can break these down to smaller levels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:34.520 --> 00:57:38.000
and say like, "Okay, this upcoming week, what&nbsp;
are the three things you want to work on?" Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:38.000 --> 00:57:42.400
with my executive assistant and our chief of&nbsp;
staff, we can help realign my schedule. Is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:42.400 --> 00:57:46.520
actually what I'm working on next week, or is it&nbsp;
a bunch of other things? One tool we stopped using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:46.520 --> 00:57:51.280
because it costs money, so we stopped paying&nbsp;
for it. But there is a tool that does this T3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:51.280 --> 00:57:54.840
B3 and then follows up, and you can have all of&nbsp;
it, but I was like, I think we can do it in Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:54.840 --> 00:57:58.160
Forms for cheaper, is Lattice.
Tim Ferriss: Lattice.&nbsp;

00:57:58.160 --> 00:58:02.027
Noah Kagan: Latticehq.com, it's&nbsp;
Sam Altman's brother, Jack.&nbsp;

00:58:02.027 --> 00:58:03.680
Tim Ferriss: Got it.
Noah Kagan: But it was 15,000 a year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:03.680 --> 00:58:08.200
and I was like, I think Google Forms is for free a&nbsp;
year, can do pretty equivalent, and it's a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:08.200 --> 00:58:11.440
more work on the team. Shout out to Charlotte,&nbsp;
and Anna, and Angelina for helping facilitate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:11.440 --> 00:58:14.920
it. But that actually will do your tracking and&nbsp;
then you could double check how you are against it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:14.920 --> 00:58:19.680
versus honestly, Google [Sheets] is comparable.
Tim Ferriss: Q1 top and singular goal. Do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:19.680 --> 00:58:22.840
you want to talk about either of these?
Noah Kagan: Yeah. So a lot of it, when you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:22.840 --> 00:58:26.160
trying to go anywhere in your business is, where&nbsp;
do you want to be by the end of the year? So right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:26.160 --> 00:58:29.080
now for anyone who's watching this, it's coming&nbsp;
out in January. Where are you going to be December&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:29.080 --> 00:58:34.480
31st, 2024, and just pick a destination with the&nbsp;
time. Most people don't do either, they're like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:34.480 --> 00:58:38.840
"Dude, you've influenced so many people on health.&nbsp;
I just want to be healthier. What does that mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:38.840 --> 00:58:42.960
to be healthier?" So be more clear on your goal&nbsp;
and your distinction. That's something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:42.960 --> 00:58:46.240
Zuckerberg, I've talked to you about it, where&nbsp;
he brought me in a room, he's like, "I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:46.240 --> 00:58:52.300
get a billion people." I was like, "Wow, that's&nbsp;
pretty cool." And this is when we had 10 million.&nbsp;

00:58:52.300 --> 00:58:54.320
Tim Ferriss: That's a big one.
Noah Kagan: That was amazing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:54.320 --> 00:58:57.640
it was exciting. And so have a very clear&nbsp;
singular goal that you want to accomplish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:57.640 --> 00:59:02.480
but maybe it's a thousand subscribers. And&nbsp;
my goals lately are much more unambitious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:02.480 --> 00:59:05.280
because I want to focus on goals I can sustain.
Tim Ferriss: Could you give an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:05.280 --> 00:59:11.920
example of what that looks like?
Noah Kagan: So with AppSumo.com, in the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:11.920 --> 00:59:15.560
if we didn't 2X a year, I'm like, "Oh, well, it&nbsp;
failed." And you'll hear this, you'll say, "Shoot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:15.560 --> 00:59:18.680
for the stars and if you're end up at the moon,&nbsp;
you're good." But also, that means I'm running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:18.680 --> 00:59:22.000
really fast and people, you burn out, because&nbsp;
you're working really hard and you're not getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:22.000 --> 00:59:26.280
where you want to go. And so now with AppSumo,&nbsp;
our general growth range is five to 10 percent a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:26.280 --> 00:59:33.120
year and so this year for 2024, I believe it'll&nbsp;
be around seven percent. And the reason that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:33.120 --> 00:59:37.316
because if I could do seven percent over&nbsp;
year, after year, after year, after year —&nbsp;

00:59:37.316 --> 00:59:39.508
Tim Ferriss: You end up the&nbsp;
Warren Buffett of AppSumo.&nbsp;

00:59:39.508 --> 00:59:42.080
Noah Kagan: Exactly. And the other thing that&nbsp;
I've noticed through these unambitious goals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:42.080 --> 00:59:47.320
and I'll give more examples, is that it creates&nbsp;
more freedom for taking 10X experiments. So we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:47.320 --> 00:59:51.040
doing a lot more content this year, by the time&nbsp;
this comes out, we're going to be trying doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:51.040 --> 00:59:55.160
an alternative to product time and some of these&nbsp;
